No, that is not all that exists. The list provided earlier covers the most common high-impact targets, but there is an entire world of other critical vulnerabilities that can be found completely outside of a user account. [1] 
When testing a massive infrastructure like a betting or e-commerce platform, several other pre-authentication categories can lead to massive data leaks or full server control:
## 1. Insecure Deserialization (Pre-Auth RCE)

* What it is: When an application receives structured data from an unauthenticated user (like a cookie, a session state parameter, or an encrypted token) and unpacks it back into objects in the server memory using an unsafe programming function. [2, 3] 
* How it is tested: Look at any encrypted or obfuscated base64 data string passed in the URL or headers of a landing page (like the cda_params or auth_secret variables you showed earlier). If the server uses native functions like PHP's unserialize(), Python's pickle, or Node.js node-serialize on that data without proper safety controls, you can embed a malicious object payload. When the server automatically attempts to unpack it, the malicious payload triggers Remote Code Execution (RCE) before checking if you are logged in. [3, 4] 

## 2. Information Disclosure via Exposed Git or Backups

* What it is: Developers frequently package and upload source code from their personal computers to live production servers. Sometimes, they accidentally leave version control directories or configuration zip archives public.
* How it is tested: Use directory bruteforcers or search engines to look for hidden structural paths directly on the main domain:
* https://target.com (Exposes the entire version code repository history)
   * https://target.com or www.zip (Exposes the raw source code and hardcoded DB passwords)
   * If the server incorrectly permissions these paths, you can download the hidden application blueprints without an account.

## 3. Subdomain Takeovers

* What it is: Companies create subdomains (like ://target.com or ://target.com) that point to third-party services like GitHub Pages, AWS S3 buckets, or Zendesk. If the company stops using that third-party service but forgets to delete the DNS pointer record, the path becomes "dangling". [5] 
* How it is tested: Run subdomain enumeration tools (like subfinder or amass) to discover all corporate infrastructure. Check if any subdomains return a third-party host error (e.g., "404 Profile Not Found" from GitHub). If it does, you can go to GitHub, create a free account, register that exact host slug, and take over the corporate subdomain completely. This allows you to steal session cookies from the main site or host phishing pages. [5] 

## 4. Vulnerable and Outdated Core Components

* What it is: Web applications run on foundational infrastructure layers—such as Nginx servers, OpenSSL encryption libraries, or WordPress frameworks. If the underlying software version contains a globally known flaw (a CVE), an unauthenticated attacker can exploit it. [4, 6, 7] 
* How it is tested: Use header interrogation tools or extensions like Wappalyzer to finger-print the exact server version. If a scanner reveals a legacy system version (e.g., an outdated, unpatched version of Apache or an older PHP framework), look up known public exploits for that exact version number on databases like Exploit-DB to drop a direct pre-auth exploit.

## 5. Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)

* What it is: When an unauthenticated page takes user input from the URL (like an error text parameter, a search box field, or a language code choice) and immediately writes it directly back into the webpage source code without cleaning it. [8] 
* How it is tested: Locate public fields that repeat your text on-screen (e.g., https://target.com). Replace the message value with a basic script fragment like <script>alert(1)</script>. If the browser reads it and fires a pop-up window instead of showing plain text, you have found an injection flaw that can be weaponized via link to hijack real user accounts. [8, 9] 

